7.1 Vector Components
Henceforth, the Einstein summation convention will be employed.
A vector can be written as a linear combination of a basis, that is a list of linearly independent vectors the span the space. We are typically interested in vectors that belong to \(\b{E}^{n}\).
We typically denote orthonormal basis vectors with a tophat, for example \(\{\hat{\bs{E}}_{i}\}_{i=1,2,3}\). Further, we will typically make use of orthonormal basis vectors unless specified otherwise.
Let \(\{\hat{\bs{E}}_{i}\}_{i=1,2,3}\) form an orthonormal basis for \(\b{E}^{3}\). That is any vector \(\bs{u} \in \b{E}^{3}\) can be written as a linear combination of these basis vectors.
\[
\begin{align}
\bs{x}&=x_1 \hat{\bs{E}}_1+ x_2 \hat{\bs{E}}_2+ x_3 \hat{\bs{E}}_3\\
\bs{x}&=x_i \hat{\bs{E}}_i
\end{align}
\]
\[
\begin{align}
[\bs{x}]_{\{\hat{\bs{E}}_{i}\}_{i=1,2,3}}&=
\left[
\begin{array}{c}
x_1\\
x_2\\
x_3
\end{array}
\right]
\end{align}
\]
Let \(\{\hat{\bs{F}}_{i}\}_{i=1,2,3}\) form another orthonormal basis for \(\b{E}^{3}\). Then,
\[
\begin{align}
\bs{x}&=\tilde{x}_1 \hat{\bs{F}}_1+ \tilde{x}_2 \hat{\bs{F}}_2+ \tilde{x}_3 \hat{\bs{F}}_3\\
\bs{x}&=\tilde{x}_i \hat{\bs{F}}_i
\end{align}
\]
\[
\begin{align}
[\bs{x}]_{\{\hat{\bs{F}}_{i}\}_{i=1,2,3}}&=
\left[
\begin{array}{c}
\tilde{x}_1\\
\tilde{x}_2\\
\tilde{x}_3
\end{array}
\right]
\end{align}
\]
Notice that the components differ when the chosen basis differs. Additionally, for clarity, the matrix representation of the components may include a subscript denoting the basis. The following are equivalent representations of the vector:
\[
\begin{align}
\bs{x} = x_{i} \hat{\bs{E}}_{i} = \tilde{x}_{i} \hat{\bs{F}}_{i}
\end{align}
\]
The question is, are the components \(x_{i}\) and \(\tilde{x}_i\) the same? In general no. However, a relation between the two represenation can be determined using a change of basis, which will be discussed in a later section.

8.1 Trace
The trace is a map that maps a tensor to a real number and obeys the following rules: For any tensor \(\bs T\) and \(\bs S\) and any vectors \(\bs a, \bs b\), \[
    \begin{align}
        \rm{tr}\pr{\bs T + \bs S} &= \rm{tr} \bs T + \rm{tr} \bs S \\
        \rm{tr}\pr{\alpha \bs T} &= \alpha ~\rm{tr} \bs T \\
        \rm{tr}\pr{\bs{a} \otimes \bs{b}} &= \bs a \cdot \bs b
    \end{align}
\]
The first two properties of the trace show that the trace is a linear map.
In terms of tensor components,
\[
\begin{align}
\text{tr} \, \bs{T} = \text{tr}({T_{ij}}\bs{e}_i\bs{e}_j) = {T_{ij}}\text{tr}(\bs{e}_i\bs{e}_j) = {T_{ij}}\bs{e}_i\bs{e}_j = {T_{ij}}{\delta_{ij}} = {T_{ii}}.
\end{align}
\]
That is,
\[
\begin{align}
\text{tr} \, \bs{T} = {T_{11}} + {T_{22}} + {T_{33}} = \text{sum of diagonal elements}.
\end{align}
\]
It is, therefore, obvious that
\[
\begin{align}
\text{tr} \, \bs{T}^T = \text{tr} \, \bs{T}
\end{align}
\]

8.2 Determinant
The determinant is also a map that maps a tensor to a real number. Here, we give a definition of the determinant that is relevant and useful in the study of continuum mechanics. Let \(\bs T\) be a tensor that maps the three vectors \(\bs a, \bs b, \bs c\). The scalar triple product of the vectors \(\bs a, \bs b, \bs c\) is given by \[
\begin{align}
\abs{\bs a \cdot (\bs b \times \bs c)}
\end{align}
\]
which represents the volume of a parallelopiped constructed by the three vectors. Then the determinant of \(\bs{T}\), denoted \(\text{det} \, \bs T\) is given by \[
\begin{align}
\text{det} \, \bs T &= \frac{\abs{\bs T \bs a \cdot (\bs T \bs b \times \bs T \bs c)}}{\abs{\bs a \cdot (\bs b \times \bs c)}} .
\end{align}
\]
In component form, the determinant of \(\bs{T}\) is the determinant of the matrix of \(\bs{T}\). \[
\begin{align}
\text{det} \, \bs T &= \text{det} \, [\bs T]
\end{align}
\]

10.3 Deformed Body and Deformed Configuration
Continuum mechanics is, for the most part, a study of deforming bodies. A reference body in the reference Euclidean space will experience forces and deform. The deformation of a reference body is described by deformation maps, which will be elucidated in a following section.
Let \(\mc{E}\) and \(\b{E}\) be referred to as the physical point space and physical Euclidean space respectively. As \(\mc{B}_{\rm{R}}\) deforms, the material particles occupy different regions in the physical point space. At time \(\tau\), \(\mc{B}\) can be mapped to a subset of \(\mc{E}\) to describe deformed body after it has changed shape or orientation. Similarly, \(\mc{B}\) can also be mapped to a subset of \(\b{E}\). That is, each material particle \(\chi \in \mc{B}\) can be mapped to a position vector in \(\b{E}\) describing its current position.
We refer to the map \(\bs{k}_{\tau} : \mc{B} \ra \bs{\mc{B}}_{\tau}\) is referred to as the current configuration or deformed configuration and the set \(\bs{\mc{B}_{\tau}} \subset \b{E}\) is called the current body or deformed body in the physical Euclidean space. \(\bs{k}_{\tau}\) is also a continuous, injective map. A material particle’s current position vector is given by \(\bs{k}_{\tau}[\chi] = \bs{x}\). We also have the map \(k_{\tau} : \mc{B} \ra \mc{B}_{\tau}\), where \(\mc{B}_{\tau}\) is the current body or deformed body in the physical point space.
Once again, to summarize the notation:

\(\bsmc{B}_{\tau} = \bs{k}_{\tau}\pr{\mc{B}} = \set{\bs{k}_{\tau}\pr{\chi} \in \b{E}}{\chi \in \mc{B}} \subset \b{E}\)   (Current body or deformed body at time \(\tau\) in \(\b{E}\))
\(\mc{B}_{\tau} = k_{\tau}\pr{\mc{B}} = \set{k_{\tau}\pr{\chi} \in \mc{E}}{\chi \in \mc{B}} \subset \mc{E}_{\tau}\)   (Current body or deformed body at time \(\tau\) in \(\mc{E}\))


10.3.1 Eulerian Coordinates
We saw that the Lagrangian coordinates describe the position of the material particles in the reference body in the reference Euclidean space. However, we require another set of coordinates to describe the position of the material particles as the body deforms. Again, assume \(\mc{E}\) and \(\b{E}\) are finite dimensional spaces and \(\b{E}\) is a Hilbert space. We select a list of vectors \(\bs{e}_{i} \in \b{E}\) that form an orthonormal basis for \(\b{E}\) and define an inner product for \(\b{E}\) such that \(\bs{e}_{i} \cdot \bs{e}_{j} = \delta_{ij}\). Let the spaces be three dimensional. We denote the material particle’s position vector in the physical Euclidean space \(\b{E}\) at time \(\tau\) with \(\bs{x}\), which can be written as a linear combination of the basis vectors:
\[
\begin{equation}
    \bs{x} = x_{1} \bs{e}_{1} + x_{2} \bs{e}_{2} + x_{3} \bs{e}_{3}
\end{equation}
\]
\(\bs{x}\) has units of distance. The scalars \(x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}\) are unitless real numbers, and the units are carried by the basis vectors \(\bs{e}_{i}\). We declare another non-dimensionalization map
\[
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{U}_{\tau} : \b{E} \ra \b{R}^3
\end{equation}
\]
defined as \[
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{U}_{\tau}[\bs{x}] = \pr{\bs{x} \cdot \bs{e}_{1}, \bs{x} \cdot \bs{e}_{2}, \bs{x} \cdot \bs{e}_{3}} = \pr{x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}}.
\end{equation}
\]
We denote the tuple \(\pr{x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}}\) with the following bold sans-serif symbol \[
\begin{equation}
\bssf{x} = \pr{x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}}
\end{equation}
\]
where \(\bssf{x} \in \b{R}^3\). Equivalently, \(\bssf{x}\) can be written as a column matrix: \[
\begin{equation}
    \bssf{x} = \begin{bmatrix}
                x_{1} \\ x_{2} \\ x_{3}
                \end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}
\]
\(\bssf{x}\) is referred to as the Eulerian coordinates or spatial coordinates of the material particle \(\bs{k}_{\rm{R}}^{-1}[\bs{X}]\) at time \(\tau\). The Eulerian coordinates are the components of \(\bs{x}\) with respect to the basis vectors \(\bs{e}_{i}\).
As the body deforms, the Eulerian coordinates of a material particle change since \(\bs{k}_{\tau}[\chi]\) occupies different positions in the physical Euclidean space.
